I said, well, let's see what you got.
我说，好吧，让我看看你有什么本事。

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:38.579
I'll be the judge of that.
我会评判一下的。

00:05:38.579 --> 00:05:39.420
I'll be the judge of that.
我会评判一下的。

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:40.980
Yeah, let's do it.
是的，来吧。

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:45.620
But, you know, I was thinking sometimes, I think that there's a tendency to dismiss TV.
但是，你知道，我有时在想，人们往往会轻视电视。

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:56.680
And I've been guilty of this too, as a kind of a popular medium that maybe, well, I think there's been so much quality television in almost novelistic ways.
我也曾犯过这种错误，认为它只是一种大众媒介，也许……但我认为现在已经有了很多具有小说质感的优质电视剧。

00:05:56.680 --> 00:06:04.720
Our show, The Wire, The Sopranos, like TV has really been elevated to high art, but it's also a populist medium, right?
我们的剧，《火线》、《黑道家族》，电视确实已经被提升到了高雅艺术的层面，但它同时也是一种大众媒介，对吧？

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:08.560
It's in everyone's home and now pockets, living rooms.
它进入了每个人的家中，现在是口袋里、客厅里。

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:23.240
But if you think about our human need for story, I'm sure people were stopping Sophocles on the street in the Algora or whatever, and being like, I don't remember what he wrote, but you know what I mean?
但如果你思考一下人类对故事的需求，我相信人们以前也会在广场上拦住索福克勒斯，说，我不记得他写了什么，但你懂我的意思吧？

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:27.279
Like saying like, I think smoking in the banded too is what he wrote.
就像说，我想他写的是《禁止吸烟》之类的。

00:06:27.279 --> 00:06:33.319
Not that I'm saying, you know, stopping a playwright agrees to sing the Orestaya Trilogy really floored me.
并不是说，你知道，拦住剧作家去唱《奥瑞斯提亚》三部曲真的让我很震撼。

00:06:33.319 --> 00:06:34.399
But I've got a few notes.
但我有几点意见。

00:06:34.399 --> 00:06:35.240
But I've got a few notes.
但我有几点意见。

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:36.279
Actually an answer for you.
其实有一个答案给你。

00:06:36.279 --> 00:06:41.639
Can you sign just, yeah, Elektra on my arm, because I'm gonna get a hat to you.
你能就在我的手臂上签个名吗，就签Elektra，因为我打算去把它纹下来。

00:06:41.639 --> 00:06:45.240
That's what people want me to write people while they answer on their arms, they get a tattoo.
人们总是让我给他们写点什么，然后他们就去纹身了。

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:51.120
But I think there is a human, I really maintain there's these human ache for stories.
但我认为人类，我始终坚持认为人类对故事有一种渴望。

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:52.480
We need them.
我们需要它们。

00:06:52.480 --> 00:07:04.720
And when people say to me like, this show changed my life, it taught me how to talk with my dad, it got me through my divorce, it gave me my sense of humor.
当人们对我说，这部剧改变了我的人生，它教会了我如何与父亲沟通，它陪我度过了离婚的低谷，它给了我幽默感。

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:11.079
I take that really seriously now, and I really can't, I continue to be moved to have been a part of something that affected people this way.
我现在非常认真地对待这些评价，我真的无法，我依然为能参与到这样影响人们的作品中而感到动容。

00:07:11.079 --> 00:07:29.800
But I also, you know, it's so strange to be watching this with my wife and watching almost every episode with her, except this episode we were separate, because I was on tour, but just to look back, it's weird when you do something on camera for this long.
但我也，你知道，和妻子一起看这部剧，几乎每一集都和她一起看，除了这一集我们分开了，因为我在巡演，这种感觉很奇妙，当你长时间在镜头前做一件事时，回首往事的感觉很怪。

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:35.600
It's almost like you have this weird moving in both senses of the word, like photo album of your life.
这就像是你的人生拥有了一本以两种含义呈现的动态相册。

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:38.439
It's like, it's this weird documentary snapshot.
就像是一个奇怪的纪录片快照。

00:07:38.439 --> 00:07:42.040
And even though that wasn't what was going on in my life, It was partly what was going on in my life.
尽管那并不是我生活中正在发生的事，但它在某种程度上确实是我生活的一部分。

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:43.600
At least that was my job.
至少那是我的工作。

