01:05:37.940 --> 01:05:39.099
He's really funny.
他真的很幽默。

01:05:39.099 --> 01:05:41.659
And it's reliably the best thing I read every week.
这绝对是我每周读到的最好的内容。

01:05:41.659 --> 01:05:46.420
And I feel like I get to sit around this campfire with Nick Cave and have him tell.
我觉得自己就像坐在尼克·凯夫身边的篝火旁，听他讲述。

01:05:46.420 --> 01:05:53.460
He wrote one today that was so funny about him and Nick Cage getting confused.
他今天写了一篇关于他和尼克·凯奇被搞混的趣事，特别好笑。

01:05:53.460 --> 01:05:57.820
And he was drinking with this guy whose wife was leaving him.
当时他正和一个妻子要离开他的男人喝酒。

01:05:57.820 --> 01:05:59.860
And he was like, my wife loves you, man.
那个人说，伙计，我妻子很喜欢你。

01:05:59.860 --> 01:06:00.860
I need to tell her.
我得告诉她。

01:06:00.860 --> 01:06:05.860
realized he thought he was Nick Cage, the actor, not Nick Cage, the rock star.
后来才意识到那个人以为他是演员尼克·凯奇，而不是摇滚明星尼克·凯夫。

01:06:05.860 --> 01:06:19.219
So he ends up writing an autograph for her, and he ends up talking about raising Arizona was a tough shoot, but the Cone Brothers is so great, and he was so drunk, just imitating Nick Cage.
于是他最后给她写了签名，还聊起《抚养亚利桑那》拍摄过程很艰苦，但科恩兄弟真的很棒，他当时喝得烂醉，一直在模仿尼克·凯奇。

01:06:19.219 --> 01:06:47.739
But I love those so much, and I think the campfires I tend to gravitate around are a little more probably movies or books, but you know, I grew up watching Cheers and Family Ties and that, you know, that Thursday lineup, that night court LA law, I mean, so I feel, I watched so much more TV when I was a kid than I do now, much to my wife, Sugarin.
但我非常喜欢这些，我觉得我倾向于围坐的“篝火”可能更多是电影或书籍，但你知道，我从小看《干杯酒吧》和《家族纽带》长大，还有周四晚上的节目阵容，比如《夜间法庭》和《洛城法网》，所以我觉得，我小时候看的电视比现在多得多，我妻子对此很有意见。

01:06:48.940 --> 01:07:00.099
But yeah, I mean, I don't think anyone gets in this business that we're in without having things that have really taken up residence in their psyche, you know?
但确实，我觉得没有人能在我们这一行立足，除非他们心里装了一些真正扎根于灵魂深处的东西，你懂吗？

01:07:00.099 --> 01:07:04.659
And I'm sure we'll end this, and I'll think of eight more in one of those.
我肯定等节目结束了，我还会想到八个这样的例子。

01:07:04.659 --> 01:07:07.500
Well, I think you hit the nail on the head by saying the word campfire.
嗯，我觉得你说到“篝火”这个词真是点睛之笔。

01:07:07.500 --> 01:07:11.300
I think Jessica feels like she was around this campfire with us for all these years.
我觉得杰西卡一定觉得这些年来她一直和我们围坐在篝火旁。

01:07:11.300 --> 01:07:12.500
We're all telling stories.
我们都在讲故事。

01:07:12.500 --> 01:07:20.739
And I think it's such a gift to have that campfire kind of burn for as many, for enough years to sort of tell enough stories around.
而且我觉得能让这堆篝火燃烧这么多年，讲足够多的故事，这真是一份礼物。

01:07:20.739 --> 01:07:22.739
And I think it's so rare to get to do that.
我觉得能做到这一点非常难得。

01:07:22.739 --> 01:07:25.500
That's why my brain went to like things I've spent lots of time with.
这就是为什么我的大脑会想到那些我花了很多时间陪伴的东西。

01:07:25.500 --> 01:07:27.059
I think that's what she's talking about.
我想这就是她所说的。

01:07:27.059 --> 01:07:28.940
So yeah, thank you Jessica for the question.
所以，谢谢杰西卡的提问。

01:07:28.940 --> 01:07:32.539
Thank you for spending that much time with us around the campfire.
谢谢你花这么多时间陪我们围坐在篝火旁。

01:07:32.539 --> 01:07:34.300
And this new campfire too.
还有这堆新的篝火。

01:07:34.300 --> 01:07:35.860
So thank you.
所以，谢谢你们。

01:07:35.860 --> 01:07:40.170
I am guilty, please equip me.
我有罪，请宽恕我。

01:07:40.170 --> 01:07:44.690
All sins are forgiven in New York City.
在纽约市，所有的罪过都会被原谅。

